{"name":"Grand Plum","hex":"#6c5657","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"grand","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Large, senior (high-ranking), intense, extreme, or exceptional\nOf a large size or extent; great.","Of a large size or extent; great."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A thousand of some unit of currency, such as dollars or pounds.","A thousand RPM."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grand"},{"term":"plum","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The fruit and its tree.\n An edible, fleshy stone fruit of species Prunus domestica (European plum) and Prunus americana American plum, often of a dark red or purple colour. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}\nA stone-fruit tree which bears this fruit, Prunus domestica. \nThe edible, fleshy stone fruit of several species resembling the plum, or the tree from which they grow. \nPrunus sect. Prunus\nPrunus cerasifera (cherry plum or myrobalan).\nPrunus salicina (Chinese plum or Japanese plum).\nPrunus spinosa (sloe).\nPrunus ursina (bear's plum).\nPrunus sect. Prunocerasus (North American plums)\nPrunus americana (American plum).\nPrunus angustifolia (Chickasaw plum or sand plum).\nPrunus hortulana (hortulan plum).\nPrunus nigra (Canadian plum or black plum).\nPrunus rivularis (creek plum or hog plum).\nPrunus subcordata (Klamath plum or Oregon plum).\nPrunus sect. Armeniaca (better known as apricots)\nPrunus mume, an Asian fruit more closely related to the apricot than the plum, usually consumed pickled, dried, or as a juice or wine; ume.\n A dried grape or raisin, as used in a pudding or cake.","An edible, fleshy stone fruit of species Prunus domestica (European plum) and Prunus americana American plum, often of a dark red or purple colour. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a dark bluish-red colour.","Choice; especially lavish or preferred."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/plum"}],"entries":[{"title":"Plum","blocks":[{"text":"A plum is a fruit of some species in Prunus subg. Prunus. Dried plums are usually called prunes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Plums_African_Rose_-_whole%2C_halved_and_slice.jpg/330px-Plums_African_Rose_-_whole%2C_halved_and_slice.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plum","wikidata":"Q12372598"},{"title":"Plum (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Plum is a purple color with a brownish-gray tinge, like that shown on the right, or a reddish purple, which is a close representation of the average color of the plum fruit."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Plum_on_tree02.jpg/330px-Plum_on_tree02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plum_(color)","wikidata":"Q3108722"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Plum_blossom_on_the_first_day_of_spring_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1214413.jpg/500px-Plum_blossom_on_the_first_day_of_spring_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1214413.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Plum_blossom_on_the_first_day_of_spring_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1214413.jpg/500px-Plum_blossom_on_the_first_day_of_spring_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1214413.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Plum flowers","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APlum_blossom_on_the_first_day_of_spring_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1214413.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/%C5%A0ljiva_Stanley_-_zeleni_plodovi.2.jpg/500px-%C5%A0ljiva_Stanley_-_zeleni_plodovi.2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/%C5%A0ljiva_Stanley_-_zeleni_plodovi.2.jpg/